          %h2#modules
            %strong Modules
          %p
            In the
            %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics#AssociatedNBundle"} previous section
            we have seen
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-actions"} ∞-actions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groups"} ∞-groups
            on objects in the ambient
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-actions"} ∞-actions
            to
            = succeed "-actions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %em additive structure
            (in generalization of the sense in which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            are additive) and where all actions are
            %em linear
            (in generalization of the sense of abelian group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+functions"} linear functions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            are called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            and hence here we discuss modules and their higher analogs.
          %p
            Notice the different role of the “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (r,n)
            ” index:
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi r
                        %mo ,
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (r,n)
                  \-ring
                %th presentation
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                  \= 1-ring =
                  = succeed "-ring" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,1)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  compatibly monoidal
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-ring"} 2-ring
                  \=
                  = succeed "-ring" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,2)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  compatibly
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  commutative
                  = succeed "-ring" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
                  \= compatibly monoidal
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group"} ∞-group
                  \/
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,2)
                  \-ring
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  compatibly
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monoidal (∞,1)-category
          %p
            A special case of modules are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            that is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            has in general no natural or useful lift to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %h3#1modules 1-Modules
          %p
            We briefly disucss here some basics of ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
            with an emphasis on the generalization from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fields"} fields
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
          %h4#physics_motivation_superposition_of_states_and_fields Physics motivation: Superposition of states and fields
          %p
            One key aspect of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            %em linear structure
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
            only, not to states in the sense of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+on+an+operator+algebra"} state on an operator algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+values"} expectation values
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            in the states as discussed here):
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition+principle"} superposition principle
                in quantum mechanics says that for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ψ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_1
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ψ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_2
                two
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
                one can form their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ψ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi ψ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_1 + \psi_2
                as well as their difference
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ψ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mi ψ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_1 - \psi_2
                such that this is again a quantum state. Moreover there is a 0-state such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo +
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mi ψ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi + 0 = \psi
                for all
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ψ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
                on the set of quantum states.
            %li
              %p
                Quantum states have
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong complex phases
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ψ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
                a quantum state and for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in \mathbb{C}
                a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+number"} complex number
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mi ψ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \psi
                and this is such that for two such complex numbers we have
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ψ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1(c_2 \psi) = (c_1 c_2) \psi
                and for two states we have
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi ψ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi ψ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi c
                        %msub
                          %mi ψ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo +
                        %mi c
                        %msub
                          %mi ψ
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c(\psi_1 + \psi_2) = c \psi_1 + c \psi_2
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
                on quantum states.
          %p
            Together this says equivalently that the additive group of quantum states is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            over the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+space"} complex vector space
          %p
            This linear structure is a crucial aspect of quanum theory. It is at the heart of phenomena such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+interference"} quantum interference
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entanglement"} entanglement
          %p
            In macroscopic physics similar behaviour is known in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+mechanics"} wave mechanics
            for freely propagating waves. Not unrelated to this is the term
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+function"} wave function
            for a quantum state. However, apart from wave mechanics, linearity is not manifest in macoscopic physical phenomena, even though it is fundamentally present; this is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decoherence"} decoherence
          %p
            It is this linear structure of quantum theory that, since shortly after its conception, led to a close relaton to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            which was unknown in classical physics. (And this came unexpected to physicist at the beginning of the 20th century and was not embraced by all theoreticians at first, see at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gruppenpest"} Gruppenpest
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+group"} symmetry group
            acts on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field configurations
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            and is preserved by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            forms a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} linear representation
            space of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+group"} symmetry group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            goes so far that under natural assumptions
            %em every
            representation of a group arises as the action of a group of symmetries on a quantum system – a relation known as the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"}> orbit method
            ” in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            have been proven by considering systems
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            this way.
          %p
            But the relation between quantum physics and linear representation theory goes deeper still. Not only do the quantum states form modules over the complex numbers, but already the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            in fundamental physics form modules over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            where for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_2
            two field configurations also
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1 + \phi_2
            is a field configuration, such that with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            a matter field for each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            there is also a matter field denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \phi
            and such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1 (f_2 \phi) = (f_1 f_2) \phi
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(\phi_1 + \phi_2) = f \phi_1 + \phi_2
          %p
            There is a more geometric expression of this: in fact the matter fields form a module with special properties (they are what is called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+module"} projective
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+generated+module"} finitely generated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre-Swan+theorem"} Serre-Swan theorem
            asserty that modules with these special properties consist of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            whose typical fiber is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            over the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+space"} complex vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundles"} associated bundles
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representations"} linear representations
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            under which the given matter fields are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charged+particle"} charged
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} linear representation
            of the relevant
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+group"} symmetry group
            actions as for the quantum states themselves: is a group acts by symmetries on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            – notably for instance the action of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+group"} Poincaré group
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            – then this translates into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} linear representation
            of the group on the module of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+functions"} pullback of functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            species with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+representation"} unitary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representations"} irreducible representations
            of the spacetime symmetry group, a relation known as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wigner+classification"} Wigner classification
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+representation+of+the+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} unitary representation of the Poincaré group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Doplicher-Roberts+reconstruction+theorem"} Doplicher-Roberts reconstruction theorem
            which shows that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superselection+sectors"} superselection sectors
            of a quantum field theory form the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+category"} representation category
            of the global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
            of a quanutm field theory on spacetime – a special case of the deep principle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
          %p
            In summary this means that the relation between quantum theory and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            theory/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"}> linear algebra
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            is intimate.
          %h4#modules_2 Modules
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
          %h4#vector_bundles Vector bundles
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
          %h3#modules_3
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            \-Modules
          %p
            We turn now to the discussion of the generalization of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#PhysicsMotivationHigherOrderStatesInQFT"} Physics motivation: Higher order states in extended QFT
          %p
            we first discuss how the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            (i.e.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"} (2,2)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-modules"} 2-modules
            and their 2-linear maps is presented by the classical 2-category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebras"} associative algebras
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
            between them and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intertwiners"} intertwiners
            between those.
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#2Modules"}> 2-Modules
              \.
          %p
            This serves as an instructive blueprint for all the following generalizations and is in itself a useful theory that neatly subsumes and organizes classical constructions and results such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
            for associative algebras, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Watts+theorem"} Eilenberg-Watts theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+theory"} invariant theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            given by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brauer+group"} Brauer group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Picard+group"} Picard group
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+units"} group of units
          %p
            Next we pass to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            proper and discuss the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-category"} (∞,2)-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} A-∞ algebras
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-bimodules"} ∞-bimodules
            internal to any compatibly cocomplete
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monoidal (∞,1)-category
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#?2Modules"}> (∞,2)-modules
              \.
          %p
            By iterating this construction in analogy to the iterative construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-categories"} (∞,n)-categories
            as
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} categories in an (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-modules"} (∞,n)-modules
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#?nModule"} (∞,n)-modules
          %h4#PhysicsMotivationHigherOrderStatesInQFT Physics motivation: Higher order states in extended QFT
          %p
            There two key classes of examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-categories"} (∞,n)-categories
            of relevance in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-categories+of+cobordisms"} (∞,n)-categories of cobordisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_n^S
            (with some specified extra structure
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+quantum+field+theory"} extended quantum field theory
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-functors"} monoidal (∞,n)-functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                    %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z \;\colon\; Bord_n^S \to \mathcal{C}
                  \\,.
          %p
            In the abstract mathematical discussion the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            here is usually allowed to be any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (∞,n)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            (notably those obtained by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            from
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+prequantum+field+theories"} extended prequantum field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
            here is special. Notably in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            1 for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{n-1}
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-1
            the value
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(\Sigma_{n-1})
            should be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{n-1}
            and thus be a
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            (a complex
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
          %p
            Therefore we expect that for relevant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories of physics
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is something that deserves to be called an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-modules"} (∞,n)-modules
            (maybe:
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-vector+spaces"} (∞,n)-vector spaces
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{C}
                  \\simeq
                  n Mod_{R}
          %p
            over some base
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            Here we discuss such
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h4#2abelian_groups 2-Abelian groups
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semigroup"} semigroup
            is a set equipped with a commutative and hence “additive” and unital pairing. A natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of addition is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            operation in a category, hence more generally the operation of taking
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                with all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            we should not try just to categorify abelian semigroups, but rather the category that these form. The category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            is notably a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            and accordingly we demand that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of 2-abelian semigroups to replace it is similarly a closed
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+2-category"} symmetric monoidal 2-category
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+categories"} presentable categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            of abelian groups are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+functions"} linear functions
            = succeed "-preserving" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
          #2Category2Ab.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p Write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo ∈
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    2 Ab \in 2Cat
            %p
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+categories"} presentable categories
              and
              = succeed "-preserving" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
              between them.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#CJF"}> CJF, def. 2.1.8
            )
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              By the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
              this is equivalently the 2-category of presentable categories and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              functors between them.
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+category"} presheaf category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\mathcal{C}}
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+category"} presentable category
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Forming presheaves on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+cocompletion"} free cocompletion
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\mathcal{C}}
              is the 2-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+abelian+group"} free abelian group
              generated by the “2-set”
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %p
              Moreover, every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+category"} presentable category
              (as discussed there) is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
              hence a left
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
              of a category of presheaves. This means that not every 2-abelian group is 2-free, but is possibly a localization of a 2-free abelian group.
          #CategoryOfModulesAs2AbelianGroup.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              Given an ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of modules
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              is presentable, hence may be regarded as a 2-abelian group. This example we discuss in more detail below in
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "BasesFor2ModulesAndTannakaDualityForAssociativeAlgebras"} Bases for 2-modules: Tannaka duality for associative algebras
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#CJF"}> CJF, example 2.1.5
            )
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              These two examples are directly analogous from the perspective of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              an
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}A
              for the corresponding 1-object
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              as its
              = succeed "-module" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi A
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Mod_A \simeq [\mathbf{B}A, Mod_R]
            %p
              is the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor+category"} enriched functor category
            %p
              Compare oth situation to how an ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+module"} free module
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              (of finite
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rank"} rank
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              is equivalently an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    N \simeq [B,R]
            %p
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The 2-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo =
                    %mi PrCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Ab = PrCat
              of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#2Category2Ab"}
              is a
              %span.newWikiWord
                closed
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/closed+monoidal+2-category"} ?
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+2-category"} symmetric monoidal 2-category
              with respect to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo ×
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo →
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \boxtimes \colon 2Ab \times 2Ab \to 2Ab
              which corepresents “bi-2-linear” functors; in that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∈
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B, C \in 2Ab
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-category"} hom-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi Ab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_{2Ab}(A \boxtimes B, C)
              is equivalently the full
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi Cat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_{Cat}(A \times B, C)
              on those that preserve
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              in each argument separately.
          %p
            See also at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pr%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29Cat"} Pr(∞,1)Cat
            for more on this.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              This is analous to the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+tensor+product+of+abelian+categories"} Deligne tensor product of abelian categories
          #SetPresheafCategorisAs2AbelianGroups.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\mathcal{C}}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+category"} presentable
              and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ⊠
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mn 2
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ×
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Set^{\mathcal{C}_1} \boxtimes
                    Set^{\mathcal{C}_2}
                    \\simeq
                    Set^{\mathcal{C}_1 \times \mathcal{C}_2}
                    \\,.
          #TensorProductModuleCategoriesAsOf2AbelianGroups.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of modules
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              is presentable and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi R
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ⊠
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi R
                          %mn 2
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi R
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msub
                          %mi R
                          %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Mod_{R_1} \boxtimes Mod_{R_2}
                    \\simeq
                    Mod_{R_1 \otimes R_2}
                    
                    \\,,
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#CJF"}> CJF, example 2.2.7
            )
          #SetsAsNonlinearModuleCategories.num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              As we discuss below in
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "BasesFor2ModulesAndTannakaDualityForAssociativeAlgebras"} Bases for 2-modules: Tannaka duality for associative algebras
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+category"} presheaf category
              in
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small category
              is a presheaf in
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            %p
              Therefore comparison of example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SetPresheafCategorisAs2AbelianGroups"}
              with example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TensorProductModuleCategoriesAsOf2AbelianGroups"}
              shows that in the context of 2-abelian semigroups plain sets play a role of a “nonlinear generalization” of linear algebra. We see this in a more pronounced way once we have introduced the notion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-modules"} 2-modules
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#2Modules"} below
              = succeed "\u201D.)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+with+one+element"} field with one element
          %h4#2rings 2-Rings
          %p
            With the ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+2-category"} monoidal 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi Ab
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Ab
            chosen, it is now straightforward to define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-rings"} 2-rings
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to that.
          #2RingAsCompatiblyMonoidalPresentableCategory.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p Write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Ring
                    %mo ∈
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    2Ring \in 2Cat
            %p
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+objects"} monoid objects
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi Ab
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 Ab
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              of this 2-category we call a
              = succeed "." do
                %strong 2-ring
            %p
              Equivalently, a 2-ring in this sense is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+category"} presentable category
              equipped with the structure of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              where the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              preserves
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#CJF"}> CJF, def. 2.1.8
            )
          .num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              The category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              with its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
              is a 2-ring and it is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi Ring
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Ring
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#CJF"}> CJF, example 2.3.4
            )
          .num_example
            %h6#example_5 Example
            %p
              The category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
              with its standard
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+abelian+groups"} tensor product of abelian groups
              is a 2-ring.
          #CommutativeRingGivesCommutative2Ring.num_example
            %h6#example_6 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              an ordinary
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              equipped with its usual
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product of modules
              is a commutative 2-ring.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              an ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              its ordinary
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of modules
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryOfModulesAs2AbelianGroup"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              is equivalently the structure of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sesquiunital+sesquialgebra"} sesquiunital sesquialgebra
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              By prop.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TensorProductModuleCategoriesAsOf2AbelianGroups"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi A
                    %mo ⊠
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Mod_A \boxtimes Mod_A \to Mod_A
            %p is equivalently a 2-linear map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Mod_{A \otimes A} \to Mod_A
                    \\,.
            %p
              By the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Watts+theorem"} Eilenberg-Watts theorem
              (which we discuss in more detail as theorem
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EilenbergWattsTheorem"}
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#MatrixCaclulusFor2Modules"} below
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes A
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Mod_R \to Mod_A
            %p
              is equivalently an
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              = succeed "-bimodule." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              is a commutative 2-ring and is canonically an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-algebra"} 2-algebra
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-ring"} 2-ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
              in that we have a canonical 2-ring homomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi ℤ
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Ab \simeq Mod_{\mathbb{Z}} \to Mod_R
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#CJF"}> CJF, example 2.3.7
            )
          %h4#2Modules 2-Modules
          #2CategoryOf2Modules.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℛ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
              a 2-ring, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#2RingAsCompatiblyMonoidalPresentableCategory"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi ℛ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    2Mod_{\mathcal{R}} \in 2Cat
            %p
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+objects"} module objects
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi Ab
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Ab
            %p
              This means that a 2-module over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+category"} presentable category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              equipped with a functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A \boxtimes N \to N
            %p which satisfies the evident action property.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#CJF"}> CJF, def. 2.3.3
            )
          #2ModulesOverThe2RingOfAnOrdinaryRing.num_example
            %h6#example_7 Example
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              be an ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              an ordinary
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} algebra
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryOfModulesAs2AbelianGroup"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_A
              is a 2-abelian group and by example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CommutativeRingGivesCommutative2Ring"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              is a commutative ring. By example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TensorProductModuleCategoriesAsOf2AbelianGroups"}
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-module"} 2-module
              structures on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_A
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo ⊠
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Mod
                      %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Mod_R \boxtimes \Mod_A \to Mod_A
            %p correspond to colimit-preserving functors
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi ℤ
                        %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Mod_{R \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}} A} \to Mod_{A}
            %p
              that satisfy the action property. Such as presented under the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Watts+theorem"} Eilenberg-Watts theorem
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EilenbergWattsTheorem"}
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi ℤ
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              itself is canonically such a bimodule and it exhibits a
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-module"} 2-module
              structure on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_A
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              Coming back to remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SetsAsNonlinearModuleCategories"}
              we observe that in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-module"} 2-module
              theory plain
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+categories"} presentable categories
              which do not “look linear” in the ordinary sense are naturally regarded as
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-modules"} 2-modules
              = succeed "-linear\u201D," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              = succeed "-linear" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_R
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-modules"} 2-modules
              = succeed "\u201D." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+with+one+element"} field with one element
          %h5#BasesFor2ModulesAndTannakaDualityForAssociativeAlgebras Bases for 2-modules: Tannaka duality for associative algebras
          %p
            The construction in example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#2ModulesOverThe2RingOfAnOrdinaryRing"}
            of 2-modules over the 2-ring induced by an ordinary commutative ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            as categories of 1-modules over some
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+module"} free module
            by a 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"}> basis
            \.
          %p To see this, write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒱
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{V} \coloneqq Mod_R
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of modules
            and regard it as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmos"} enriching context
            for
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
          %p
            So a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            serves as a
            %em
              2-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
            for a free
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-module"} 2-module
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is equivalently a (pointed)
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} = \mathbf{B}A
            with a single object (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            may be called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebroid"} algebroid
          %p
            Since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            in the category underlying a
            %span.newWikiWord
              2-abelian group
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/2-abelian+group"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorify
            the sum operation in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            the 2-analog of the free module on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+cocompletion"} free cocompletion
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            as a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+category"} presheaf category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor+category"} enriched functor category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒱
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒱
                  %mi Cat
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{V}]
                  \\in
                  \\mathcal{V}Cat
                  \\,.
          %p
            Compare this to how for a (finite dimensional) vector space a presentation by a basis is a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            such that the vector space is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Maps
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi Vect
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Maps(B,k)
                  \\in
                  Vect_k
          %p
            Indeed, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} = \mathbf{B}A
            this is the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of modules
            over
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒱
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\mathbf{B}A, \mathcal{V}]
                  \\simeq
                  Mod_A
                  \\,.
          %p
            In fact in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            it is customary generally to call
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functors"} enriched functors
            into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒱
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
            “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"}> modules
            ” (see there).
          %p
            Without further assumptions on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒱
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{V}]
            is just a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-module"} 2-module
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            is equipped with further
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            and/or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property"} property
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒱
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{V}]
            inherits further property. This relation between types of algebras and types of monoidal categories is known as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
          %p The following table lists the main items of the disctionary of Tannaka duality for associtive algebras that are used in the literature.
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
                for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
                over
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebras"} associative algebras
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"}> monoid
                    \/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of modules
              %tbody
